The purpose of this position is to supervise Associates responsible to monitor compliance in terms of the requirements of the Currency and Exchanges Manual for Authorised Dealers, Exchange Control Regulations, policies and procedures.
Detailed Description
The successful candidate will, among others duties, be responsible for the following key performance areas:
To ensure compliance and reporting of cross-border transactions to prevent the abuse of the financial system and support regulation of financial institutions.
To adjudicate and sign exchange control correspondence/applications within the defined signing limit, and adhere to Bank policies, standards and laid down procedures, including control procedures aimed at mitigating risks.
To enhance risk-based supervisory practices by embedding a data-driven decision-making culture
To embed and enhance processes and systems to reduce inefficiencies on monitoring exceptions.
To supervise the preparation and finalisation of statistical reports and management information.
To engage and/or manages internal and external stakeholders.
To independently deliver high quality work, display a service orientation, prepares solutions that reflect the range of knowledge, methods/practices in the execution of tasks and activities.
To embrace and leverage analytical tools to automate processes.
To plan, manage and prioritise the delivery of own output, coordinate the deliverables of Associates and guide Associates in the execution of the division’s activities.
To provide input to Associates' performance evaluation and development needs.
To embed the SARB culture and manage change and innovation.
Job Requirements
To be considered for this position, candidates must have:
A minimum of a Bachelor’s degree in Banking, Finance, Statistics, Business Science OR equivalent NQF level 7 qualification; and
five to eight years’ relevant working experience in the financial/banking or regulatory sector.
Additional requirements include:
knowledge of the Currency and Exchanges Manual for Authorised Dealers;
knowledge of the Exchange Control Regulations;
knowledge of relevant risk management practices;
an understanding of the key regulatory requirements facing financial institutions and related entities;
experience in using analytical tools such as PowerBI;
experience in leading teams;
service and stakeholder focus;
building and maintaining work relationships;
drive for results;
problem-solving and analytical skills;
financial and business acumen.

Duties and Accountabilities
Review and analyze sophisticated financial models.
Perform company financial analysis from a credit perspective.
Draft memoranda, reports, presentations and other documentation required for IFC internal supervision processes.
Engage cross departmental staff (including Industry teams, Legal, Financial Operations, Credit, Syndications, etc.) as well as clients in connection to project work.
Conduct industry and market research. Participate in restructuring/rescheduling and investment recovery negotiations.
Maintain CSO knowledge management platforms, ensuring that project and other information is complete and up to date.
Selection Criteria
The successful candidates would be expected to have the following general attributes:
Academic background in finance, with an MBA or its equivalent;
Minimum of 5 years of relevant work experience, preferably in banking, consulting, or a relevant industry role. Experience in restructuring work and exposure to multiple industry sectors are a plus;
Outstanding analytical skills: ability to analyze financial statements; build complex financial models; and evaluate/recommend transaction structures to achieve IFC’s objectives.
Strong negotiation and transaction execution skills;
Sound business judgment and strong analytical skills;
Demonstrated passion for working internationally in developing countries and ability to travel accordingly;
Ability to build effective relationships with IFC’s investee clients;
Clear and nuanced oral and written communication skills including demonstrated ability to communicate complex ideas clearly and tactfully to a variety of stakeholders; and
Demonstrated teamwork skills with the potential to grow into leadership roles.

The main purpose of this position is to secure sources of supply and to manage the complex sourcing of operations-related goods and services, including legal, financial advisory, training and other types of consultancy services on behalf of the South African Reserve Bank (SARB).
The SARB is looking to appoint a candidate that has extensive experience in leading cross-functional teams in the execution of strategic sourcing initiatives and tender management processes.
Detailed Description
The successful candidate will be responsible for the following key performance areas:
Define own work plan and deliverables, with guidance from the Category Lead.
Source operations-related goods and services in adherence to the SARB’s Procurement Policy and relevant legislation, including the Broad-based Black Economic Empowerment Act 53 of 2003 (B-BBEE Act).
Source operations-related goods and services, which includes, but is not limited to, market research, the preparation of requests for tender/proposal/quotation/information (RFXs), evaluation of bids, commercial negotiations, recommendations for supplier awarding as well as the preparation and presentation of submissions to relevant committees.
Manage the entire value chain of requisitioning, including the contracting cycle.
Collate and analyse spend data for the execution of operations category management initiatives and the provision of management information.
Keep abreast of developments in the procurement field to ensure best practices in the SARB.
Keep abreast of technology advancements and their effect on the costs of goods and services to be procured.
Perform spend analyses and formulate category strategies that will lead to benefits (cost reduction or cost avoidance) without causing supply risks.
Ensure that the contracts between the SARB and suppliers are commercially sound.
Job Requirements
To be considered for this position, candidates must have:
a minimum of a Bachelor’s Degree in Commerce or Supply Chain Management, or an equivalent qualification;
a minimum of 5–8 years of experience in the procurement or supply chain management environment, of which 2–3 years are related to strategic/complex sourcing ; and
Chartered Institute of Purchasing and Supply (CIPS) certification would be an added advantage.
Additional requirements include:
knowledge and skill in:
industry, organisational and business awareness;
quality assurance;
continuous improvement;
continued learning and/or professional development;
